<BaseLayout title="RevDev | Marcelo Ellwanger" description="RevDev is a native .NET 8/WPF control surface for durable, guarded multi-project AI-assisted development via Dev Relay.">
  <section class="case-hero case-hero-featured wrap">
    <div class="case-title-block"><p class="eyebrow">Developer infrastructure · Active development</p><h1>RevDev</h1><p class="case-copy">A native Windows control surface for durable multi-project AI-assisted development via Dev Relay—built to keep execution guarded, state visible, and long-running engineering work resumable across projects, chats and model boundaries.</p><div class="case-actions"><a class="button" href="#architecture">Explore the system ↓</a></div></div>
    <div class="case-status-panel mono" aria-label="RevDev project status"><div class="case-status-head"><span>PROJECT / STATE</span><b>ACTIVE DEVELOPMENT</b></div><div class="case-status-row"><span>Platform</span><strong>.NET 8 · WPF</strong></div><div class="case-status-row"><span>Authority</span><strong>DEV RELAY</strong></div><div class="case-status-row"><span>Tool hub</span><strong>5 PROVIDERS</strong></div><div class="case-status-row"><span>Safety</span><strong>LOCAL-FIRST · HOSTED_DENY</strong></div><div class="case-status-foot"><span class="status-dot">FINAL ACCEPTANCE IN PROGRESS</span></div></div>
  </section>
  <section class="case-section wrap case-intro-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>AI-assisted development needs durable state, not chat memory.</h2></div><div class="case-long-copy"><p>Working across several repositories with AI assistants creates a coordination problem: goals drift, long operations lose visibility, failures repeat, and a new chat or model can inherit only fragments of what happened.</p><p>RevDev makes that work visible and resumable without becoming the execution authority. It is the operator-facing Windows surface; Dev Relay remains the guarded backend that validates branch/HEAD state, executes bounded missions and records exact results.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap" id="architecture"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Architecture boundary</p><h2>Control surface and execution authority stay separate.</h2><p>The UI can show state, queue guarded work and expose typed product actions, but it does not turn into an arbitrary shell or silently bypass Dev Relay's safety contracts.</p></div><div class="rules-table"><div class="rule-row"><span>CONTROL SURFACE</span><strong>Native .NET 8 / WPF</strong><b>REVDEV</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>EXECUTION</span><strong>Guarded mission runner + exact terminal results</strong><b>DEV RELAY</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>CONTINUITY</span><strong>Git + exact results + CURRENT_GOALS</strong><b>DURABLE</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>HOSTED WORK</span><strong>Private development defaults to local validation</strong><b>HOSTED_DENY</b></div></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Durable continuity</p><h2>A fresh agent can resume from machine-readable truth.</h2><p>Project goals, exact results, evidence pointers and repository state live outside the conversation. That makes continuation possible across ChatGPT tiers, other hosted models, local agents or a human operator without reconstructing the project from memory.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Tool hub</p><h2>Five products, one bounded operator surface.</h2><p>RevDev integrates ExactArtifact, GapTrace, VektorDeck, CrashScope and EasyFlix through explicit provider contracts. Typed actions can open evidence or invoke product-owned workflows without merging repositories or exposing an unrestricted command surface.</p></div><div class="rules-table"><div class="rule-row"><span>ExactArtifact</span><strong>Artifact integrity / deterministic manifests</strong><b>PROVIDER</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>GapTrace</span><strong>Network-health evidence</strong><b>PROVIDER</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>VektorDeck</span><strong>Local-AI workstation tooling</strong><b>PROVIDER</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>CrashScope</span><strong>Diagnostics / evidence access</strong><b>PROVIDER</b></div><div class="rule-row"><span>EasyFlix</span><strong>Typed media-handoff import</strong><b>PROVIDER</b></div></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Progress + failure learning</p><h2>One user action can stay understandable across many internal operations.</h2><p>The current architecture models grouped multi-step interaction cycles with plan, progress and liveness state, restart-safe persistence and one terminal outcome so a long cycle can remain visibly active without producing duplicate completion signals.</p></div><div class="architecture-note mono"><span>FAILURE MODEL</span><p>Structured outcomes, recurrence-aware classification and evidence integrity turn repeated automation failures into explicit recovery rules instead of repeated guesswork.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Windows lifecycle</p><h2>Packaging is part of the system.</h2><p>RevDev has a self-contained Windows x64 package, guarded installer/update/uninstall lifecycle, integrity manifests and a canonical installed-app/shortcut identity. Final restart acceptance and release-lifecycle qualification are still in progress.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section wrap"><div class="case-section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Screenshots</p><h2>Real final-state captures only.</h2><p>The public screenshot set is intentionally pending the final acceptance pass. No mock dashboard is substituted here; the future dashboard, live-progress and tool-hub captures will come from the accepted normal installed state and will be sanitized before publication.</p></div></section>
  <section class="case-section case-closing wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Current boundary</p><h2>ACTIVE DEVELOPMENT — not a public release.</h2><p>The durable architecture is portfolio-ready, but public release remains intentionally gated on final runtime, packaging, screenshot and publication acceptance. Repository publication, license selection, release tags and LinkedIn launch remain explicit approval gates.</p><div class="case-actions"><a class="button" href="/">Back to portfolio ↑</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
